🗓️ WEEK 2 — SYSTEM DESIGN AND ARCHITECTURE

Date: Monday, 17 August 2026

🎯 Objective

The objective of Week 2 was to design the overall system: the architecture, the main modules, the API endpoints and the first UML diagrams.

🛠️ Work Completed
Designed a three-tier client–server architecture:
Presentation tier: two React web apps (Customer app and Cash desk)
Application tier: one Express REST API
Data tier: SQLite database and the Razorpay gateway
Divided the backend into services:
auth
cart (sessions)
pricing
checkout
cash desk
Listed the main API endpoints:
POST /auth/customer/login
POST /cart/:id/add
POST /razorpay/orders
POST /razorpay/verify
POST /pos/bills/:id/collect
Drew the first use case and class diagrams.
Chose the technology stack: React, Vite, Tailwind CSS, Node.js, Express, SQLite and Razorpay.

⚠️ Error / Problem Faced

In our first design, the phone calculated the bill total and sent that amount to the server for payment.

While drawing the sequence diagram we realised that anyone could edit this request in the browser and pay Rs. 1 for a Rs. 3,000 shirt.

We were also unsure whether "Pay online" and "Pay cash" should be «include» or «extend» in the use case diagram.

✅ Resolution
Moved all pricing to the server.
The app now sends only the barcode and the quantity.
The server looks up the price and GST from the database and creates the payment order itself.
Wrote this down as our main design rule: clients send intents, never amounts.
Made the two payment methods «extend» Checkout, because the customer picks only one.
Made "Verify payment signature" «include» Pay online, because it always happens.

🖼️ Project Picture

Three-tier architecture of FlashCart: browser apps, Node.js server, and the data and payment services.

💡 Learning

We learned that security decisions belong in the design phase, not after coding.

We also learned that drawing a sequence diagram early exposes problems that are hard to see in a block diagram.

